course: dictionary
lesson: 13842
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning the author wants by reading the words around it. This is context, and it is your best clue. «The jib flapped as the boat turned into the wind.» A boat has no arms, so jib here must be a part of the boat.
explain: Jib can also be a part of a crane. «The crane's jib swung slowly over the building site.» A crane's jib is the long arm that sticks out and holds the hook. Try your meaning in the sentence: a long arm swinging over the site makes sense.
explain: Jib can be a verb too, meaning to refuse or be unwilling to do something. «The donkey jibbed at the bridge and would not cross.» A donkey that stops and will not move forward is jibbing. Swap in refused: the donkey refused at the bridge and would not cross. It still makes sense.
fact: Jib (noun) means a triangle-shaped sail at the front of a sailing boat.
fact: Jib (noun) can also mean the long arm of a crane that sticks out and holds the hook.
fact: Jib (verb) means to refuse or be unwilling to do something, like a horse that stops and will not go on.
fact: «The cut of his jib» is an old phrase meaning how someone looks or seems.
fact: When a word has more than one meaning, check the context to find the right one.
exercise: «The jib pulled tight as the boat turned into the wind.» What is a jib in this sentence? | A kind of wooden bridge | A bag for carrying fish | A long, thin fishing rod | *A triangle-shaped sail at the front of a boat
exercise: «The crane's jib swung slowly over the street.» Where is the jib? | Inside a cupboard | *On the crane, as a long arm holding a hook | Down in the sea | Under the road
exercise: «The donkey jibbed at the bridge and would not cross.» What does jibbed mean? | Fell fast asleep | Ran across quickly | Sang a loud song | *Refused to go on, or was unwilling
test: The old captain looked at the boy and said, «I don't like the cut of your jib.» What does the phrase suggest? | *Did not like how the boy looked or seemed | Thought the boy's sail was very fine | Asked about the boy's sailing boat | Wanted the boy to buy a new hat
test: Meera's dad jibbed at paying so much for the tickets. What does jibbed suggest? | Paid happily straight away | *Was unwilling to pay | Lost his wallet on the way | Counted the coins twice
exam: «The wind filled the jib and the little boat leapt forward.» Which meaning of jib fits? | The long arm of a crane | A hook on a fishing line | *A triangle-shaped sail at the front of a boat | To refuse to do something
